WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.653 --> 00:00:08.889
Bitcoin has no accounts or balances.

2
00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:12.618
It has coins that move from one transaction to the next.

3
00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:18.111
Each transaction spends old coins, its inputs, and creates new coins, its outputs.

4
00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:22.797
This one, for example, has one input on the left and two outputs on the right.

5
00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:26.915
An output that has not been spent yet is called a UTXO.

6
00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:31.338
When an output is spent, it becomes the input of another transaction.

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.888
That forms a chain. The graph draws it:

8
00:00:33.888 --> 00:00:37.234
each box is a transaction, and each line is a coin.

9
00:00:37.387 --> 00:00:40.218
A chain analyst walks this graph to follow the money.

10
00:00:40.218 --> 00:00:42.605
Here you can see what that analyst sees.

11
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.184
There are two ways to open the graph. The first:

12
00:00:49.184 --> 00:00:51.506
scan a transaction on the home page.

13
00:00:51.853 --> 00:00:55.432
In the result, the Analyst tools section includes the graph,

14
00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:58.008
already expanded by the chain analysis.

15
00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:03.232
The orange box is the scanned transaction. Around it are the ones before and after.

16
00:01:03.387 --> 00:01:06.322
This button opens it full screen, with every tool.

17
00:01:06.487 --> 00:01:09.485
The second way is the Graph page, in the top menu.

18
00:01:09.485 --> 00:01:12.493
From a result, it opens that same transaction.

19
00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:17.630
If you open it without a transaction, it shows your last saved graph, or an example.

20
00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:21.410
Paste a transaction ID into the search box and press Enter.

21
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.441
The slash key focuses it too.

22
00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.993
Each box is a node, that is, a transaction.

23
00:01:30.153 --> 00:01:32.073
At the top is its shortened ID.

24
00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:36.348
Below, how many inputs and outputs it has, and the total in sats.

25
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.117
The last line says what kind of transaction it looks like:

26
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:42.910
simple send, batch send or consolidation.

27
00:01:43.087 --> 00:01:47.168
An orange border marks the root, the transaction you started from.

28
00:01:47.168 --> 00:01:49.398
Blue is an ordinary transaction.

29
00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:55.133
Cyan means a known entity, with its name inside the node. Here, the Huobi exchange.

30
00:01:55.287 --> 00:01:59.861
And green is a CoinJoin, tagged with its protocol. You will see one later.

31
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.122
Hovering over a node shows a summary, with its fee rate.

32
00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:06.918
The legend, at the top left, lists every color. Keep it handy.

33
00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:13.056
The plus buttons grow the graph.

34
00:02:13.056 --> 00:02:16.743
The left one brings in the transaction that created an input.

35
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.228
The right one brings in the transaction that spent an output.

36
00:02:20.228 --> 00:02:23.028
If nothing spent it, there is nothing to bring.

37
00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:27.903
Each click adds a node. This counter shows how many there are, up to two hundred.

38
00:02:28.053 --> 00:02:32.740
Double-clicking a node expands up to five inputs and five outputs at once.

39
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.551
A node's X collapses it, together with whatever hangs from it.

40
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.579
Undo reverts the last step. Reset goes back to just the root.

41
00:02:40.753 --> 00:02:44.601
Drag the background to pan. The wheel, or plus and minus, zoom.

42
00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:46.195
Fit frames everything.

43
00:02:46.353 --> 00:02:49.480
You can also drag a node to place it wherever you like.

44
00:02:49.653 --> 00:02:52.017
Almost everything has a keyboard shortcut.

45
00:02:52.017 --> 00:02:54.631
Hover a button to see its key in brackets.

46
00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:03.236
Each line is a coin: an output of one transaction, spent in the next.

47
00:03:03.387 --> 00:03:08.271
Its color shows the address type. Green for legacy addresses, P2PKH.

48
00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:11.107
Blue for SegWit, purple for Taproot.

49
00:03:11.287 --> 00:03:15.687
Its thickness depends on the amount: the more sats it carries, the thicker it is.

50
00:03:15.853 --> 00:03:18.841
A glowing red stroke marks a deterministic chain:

51
00:03:18.841 --> 00:03:22.872
links that can be followed with full certainty, hop after hop.

52
00:03:23.053 --> 00:03:26.664
The Edges button, or the L key, changes what the lines show:

53
00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:29.081
script type, linkability or entropy.

54
00:03:29.253 --> 00:03:32.065
Linkability colors each line by the probability

55
00:03:32.065 --> 00:03:34.648
that a specific input paid that output.

56
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.196
Entropy colors it by how many interpretations the transaction allows.

57
00:03:39.196 --> 00:03:41.158
More entropy, more privacy.

58
00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.850
And when three or more outputs of one transaction are spent together in another,

59
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.704
they merge into one line with a times N badge.

60
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.645
Click a node and it opens in port mode:

61
00:03:54.645 --> 00:03:57.816
each input on the left, each output on the right.

62
00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:01.245
Now the lines leave from the exact output that was spent.

63
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.668
On the right, the details panel opens, with a quick score:

64
00:04:04.668 --> 00:04:07.046
here, F, twenty-four out of a hundred.

65
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.367
That grade uses only this transaction's heuristics.

66
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:12.961
The full scan adds the chain analysis.

67
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.968
The I/O tab lists inputs and outputs, with address and amount.

68
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.262
The badges on top come from the Boltzmann analysis:

69
00:04:20.262 --> 00:04:24.023
bits of entropy, deterministic links and interpretations.

70
00:04:24.187 --> 00:04:26.263
This output carries the change mark.

71
00:04:26.263 --> 00:04:29.974
The box on the right lets you mark or unmark change by hand.

72
00:04:30.153 --> 00:04:32.895
Trace follows the change forward automatically.

73
00:04:32.895 --> 00:04:35.653
Link trace follows the linkability instead.

74
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.919
Here it stops right away, and says why: the output has not been spent.

75
00:04:40.087 --> 00:04:43.707
The Analysis tab lists the problems and the positives found.

76
00:04:43.887 --> 00:04:47.413
Technical shows version, locktime, size, fee and block.

77
00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:51.291
At the bottom you can find a connection from here, open the full scan,

78
00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:53.181
or make this node the new root.

79
00:04:53.353 --> 00:04:55.946
Another click on the node closes port mode.

80
00:04:59.553 --> 00:05:03.396
Now, the important part: what this graph reveals about its owner.

81
00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.794
This transaction pays three million sats,

82
00:05:05.794 --> 00:05:09.213
and sends the rest back to the same address that spent it.

83
00:05:09.387 --> 00:05:10.646
That is address reuse.

84
00:05:10.646 --> 00:05:14.787
Anyone can tell which output is the payment and which is the change.

85
00:05:14.953 --> 00:05:19.929
That is why Boltzmann finds a single interpretation and zero bits of entropy.

86
00:05:19.929 --> 00:05:21.988
Every link is deterministic.

87
00:05:22.153 --> 00:05:24.467
Going back, the coins come from Huobi.

88
00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:28.006
An exchange knows which customer each withdrawal paid.

89
00:05:28.187 --> 00:05:29.021
Going forward,

90
00:05:29.021 --> 00:05:34.160
the recipient merges this payment with thirty-nine other coins in a consolidation.

91
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.487
Spending coins together suggests they share an owner.

92
00:05:37.487 --> 00:05:40.426
That is the common input ownership heuristic.

93
00:05:40.587 --> 00:05:42.924
Now compare it with a Whirlpool CoinJoin.

94
00:05:44.653 --> 00:05:49.173
Five inputs, and five identical outputs of two and a half million sats.

95
00:05:49.173 --> 00:05:52.414
The earlier node, in green, is also a CoinJoin.

96
00:05:52.587 --> 00:05:59.828
Boltzmann finds 1,496 interpretations: 10.55 bits of entropy.

97
00:05:59.987 --> 00:06:02.868
No output can be tied to an input with certainty.

98
00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:06.234
That is how a CoinJoin breaks the links in the graph.

99
00:06:06.387 --> 00:06:09.562
Remember: deterministic means the link is certain.

100
00:06:09.562 --> 00:06:13.177
Ambiguous means there are many possible explanations.

101
00:06:13.353 --> 00:06:16.233
The Heat map colors each node by its privacy score.

102
00:06:16.387 --> 00:06:20.881
And Fingerprint changes each node's shape and color by its version and locktime,

103
00:06:20.881 --> 00:06:23.209
traces that can give away the wallet.

104
00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:31.806
Are two transactions connected? Find connection answers that question.

105
00:06:31.987 --> 00:06:36.094
It opens from the toolbar, or from a node. From is filled in with the root.

106
00:06:36.253 --> 00:06:38.400
In To, paste a txid or an address.

107
00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.123
The crosshair button lets you pick a node in the graph.

108
00:06:42.287 --> 00:06:46.661
Budgets tune the search: hops per side, transactions fetched, and seconds.

109
00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.983
By default it stops at CoinJoins, because crossing one proves no link.

110
00:06:51.153 --> 00:06:55.840
Each fetched transaction is a request to mempool.space or to your own node.

111
00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.146
Here, To is a transaction three hops ahead, which is not in the graph.

112
00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:05.123
The summary says where the search stopped, and what it could not cross.

113
00:07:05.287 --> 00:07:07.686
It found a path of the kind From funds To.

114
00:07:07.853 --> 00:07:13.136
Other possible kinds are: direct spend, shared ancestor, and spent together later.

115
00:07:13.287 --> 00:07:17.656
Each hop shows its probability. The first is certain: one hundred percent.

116
00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:22.490
The next one drops to one percent: that transaction has eighty-seven inputs.

117
00:07:22.653 --> 00:07:26.078
The weakest hop sets the path's tier. Here, Ambiguous.

118
00:07:26.253 --> 00:07:30.437
The tiers go from deterministic, to likely, weak and ambiguous.

119
00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:35.588
Without a Boltzmann matrix for a hop, its value is an estimate, and a note says so.

120
00:07:35.753 --> 00:07:39.683
Add to graph brings in the whole path in one step, which you can undo.

121
00:07:39.853 --> 00:07:42.472
Careful: no path does not mean no connection.

122
00:07:42.472 --> 00:07:47.434
Only that none was found within those budgets. That is what Search deeper is for.

123
00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.828
The Time button, or the T key, orders the nodes by block height.

124
00:07:54.987 --> 00:07:57.326
Each column shows its block number and date.

125
00:07:57.487 --> 00:08:00.943
Between columns, a marker says how many blocks were skipped.

126
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.268
Here you can see the Huobi withdrawal happened in February,

127
00:08:04.268 --> 00:08:06.759
and the payment almost three months later.

128
00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.634
Press Time again to go back to the hop layout.

129
00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.782
Control-click, or Shift-click, selects several nodes at once.

130
00:08:16.953 --> 00:08:20.511
On touch screens, turn on this button and every tap selects.

131
00:08:20.687 --> 00:08:24.284
A bar appears at the bottom, with actions for the whole selection.

132
00:08:24.453 --> 00:08:28.588
Label gives them all the same name. Here, the two Huobi nodes.

133
00:08:28.753 --> 00:08:33.403
Isolate keeps only the selection and its neighbors in view, one or two hops away.

134
00:08:33.587 --> 00:08:37.519
Each active filter shows up top as a chip. Its X removes it.

135
00:08:37.687 --> 00:08:40.336
Hide takes nodes out of view without deleting them.

136
00:08:40.336 --> 00:08:42.456
The chip counts how many are hidden.

137
00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:47.052
Remove deletes them from the graph. The root can never be removed or hidden.

138
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.819
In the legend, a click on Standard or CoinJoin hides that kind of node.

139
00:08:55.953 --> 00:08:59.313
To write on the graph, turn on Annotate, or press the A key.

140
00:08:59.487 --> 00:09:02.437
Click a node to give it a label. Enter saves it.

141
00:09:02.587 --> 00:09:05.842
The circles on the lines let you label a specific coin.

142
00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.533
And a click on the background creates a note.

143
00:09:08.533 --> 00:09:11.129
Double-click it to write a title and text.

144
00:09:11.287 --> 00:09:14.600
Labels and notes are saved with the graph, in your browser.

145
00:09:18.187 --> 00:09:23.371
Save, or the S key, stores the graph under a name. It stays in your browser only.

146
00:09:23.553 --> 00:09:25.175
Open lists your saved graphs.

147
00:09:25.175 --> 00:09:28.836
And when you come back to the Graph page, the last one opens.

148
00:09:28.987 --> 00:09:31.790
Share copies a link that carries the graph inside it.

149
00:09:32.953 --> 00:09:34.252
Think before sending it:

150
00:09:34.252 --> 00:09:38.235
whoever opens it sees exactly which transactions you were looking at.

151
00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.479
The Save panel also exports your labels in BIP329 format.

152
00:09:46.653 --> 00:09:51.669
BIP329 is a standard for moving labels from one wallet to another.

153
00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:56.766
Careful: the file is plain text. Anyone who has it can read all your labels.

154
00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.937
Import reads such a file. If it clashes with your labels, you see a review first.

155
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.777
It counts the new labels, the unchanged ones and the conflicts.

156
00:10:05.953 --> 00:10:10.054
In each conflict your label is kept, unless you tick the incoming one.

157
00:10:10.054 --> 00:10:11.828
Then you apply the import.

158
00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:19.284
To finish, all of it together, with the same payment, from scratch.

159
00:10:19.453 --> 00:10:24.695
Backwards: the coins come from a Huobi withdrawal. The exchange knows its customer.

160
00:10:24.853 --> 00:10:27.790
In the middle, the change goes back to the same address:

161
00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.033
payment and change are in plain sight.

162
00:10:30.187 --> 00:10:33.776
Forwards, the payment ends up in the recipient's consolidation.

163
00:10:33.953 --> 00:10:37.495
Find connection reaches further, but through an ambiguous hop.

164
00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:43.457
What was found gets labeled, and the graph is saved to continue another day.

165
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:45.196
The lesson:

166
00:10:45.196 --> 00:10:50.175
an identified withdrawal, a reused address and obvious change make a clear trail.

167
00:10:50.175 --> 00:10:52.391
A CoinJoin would have broken it.

168
00:10:56.187 --> 00:10:59.543
Recap. Nodes are transactions, and lines are coins.

169
00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.901
Plus expands, a click opens the ports and details, and Edges shows linkability.

170
00:11:05.053 --> 00:11:09.229
Find connection, Time, selection and labels help you investigate.

171
00:11:09.387 --> 00:11:12.001
And the whole analysis runs in your browser.

172
00:11:13.853 --> 00:11:15.630
Try it at am-i.exposed.
